 100. Let's compute that. \n\n9 divided by 450. Let's see, 450 divided by 9 is 50. So, 9 divided by 450 is 1/50, which is 0.02, so 2%. \n\nWait, so both methods give the same answer. So, that's reassuring. So, whether I compute in inches or convert to feet first, I get 2%. \n\nTherefore, the answer is 2%. \n\nBut let me just make sure there's no trick here. The problem says \"the combined length of the remoras.\" So, the remoras are attached to the shark, but are they part of the body length? Wait, the problem says \"the combined length of the remoras.\" So, does that mean the total length of the remoras is compared to the total body length of the shark? \n\nYes, that's how it's phrased. So, the remoras are attached to the shark, but their combined length is a portion of the shark's body length. So, the answer is 2%. \n\nAlternatively, if the remoras were part of the body, but that seems unlikely. Typically, remoras are tail fin-like structures, so they are attached to the tail, which is part of the body. So, their combined length is part of the total body length. So, yes, 2% is correct. \n\nTherefore, I think the answer is 2%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe combined length of the remoras is \\boxed{2\\%} of the shark's body length.\n</think>\n\nHaruka saw a 450-foot shark with 4 remoras, each 27 inches long. To find the percentage of the shark's body length that the combined length of the remoras represents, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total length of the remoras:**\n   \\[\n   4 \\text{ remoras} \\times 27 \\text{ inches/remora} = 108 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Convert the shark's body length from feet to inches:**\n   \\[\n   450 \\text{ feet} \\times 12 \\text{ inches/foot} = 5400 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the percentage of the shark's body length that the combined remoras length represents:**\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{108 \\text{ inches}}{5400 \\text{ inches}} \\right) \\times 100 = \\left( \\frac{108}{5400} \\right) \\times 100 = \\left( \\frac{1}{50} \\right) \\times 100 = 2\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the combined length of the remoras is \\boxed{2\\%} of the shark's body length.", "model_answer": 2.0, "ground_truth": 2.0, "is_correct": true}
