0. Hmm, 84 divided by 840. Well, 840 divided by 84 is 10, so 84 divided by 840 is 1/10, which is 0.1. Then, multiplying by 100 gives 10%. So, the combined remoras make up 10% of the shark's body length.\n\nWait, let me double-check that. If the shark is 70 feet long, which is 840 inches, and the remoras are 42 inches each, two of them would be 84 inches. So, 84 divided by 840 is indeed 0.1, which is 10%. That seems right. Let me confirm with another method. If 10% of 840 is 84, then yes, because 10% is 84. So, 84 is 10% of 840. Therefore, the answer is 10%.\n\nBut just to make sure I didn't make any mistakes in unit conversion. The shark's length was 70 feet. 70 feet to inches: 70*12=840 inches. Correct. Each remora is 42 inches, two of them: 42*2=84 inches. Correct. Then, 84/840=0.1=10%. All steps check out. I think that's solid. No errors here.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe combined length of the remoras is \\boxed{10\\%} of the shark's body length.\n</think>\n\nArjun saw a 70-foot shark with 2 remoras, each 42 inches long. To find the percentage of the shark's body length that is the combined length of the remoras, we first convert the shark's length to inches:\n\n\\[\n70 \\text{ feet} \\times 12 \\text{ inches per foot} = 840 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the combined length of the remoras. Each remora is 42 inches, so for 2 remoras:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 42 \\text{ inches} = 84 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nTo find the percentage of the shark's body length that is the combined remoras, we use the formula for percentage:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{84 \\text{ inches}}{840 \\text{ inches}} \\right) \\times 100 = 0.1 \\times 100 = 10\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the combined length of the remoras is \\boxed{10\\%} of the shark's body length.", "model_answer": 10.0, "ground_truth": 10.0, "is_correct": true}
