mile. Then, for 1 mile, 50/77 minutes. Therefore, for 77 miles, 77*(50/77) =", "model_answer": 77.0, "ground_truth": 50.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 51, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 176 minutes to cover every 35 miles of the city. If the city is 70 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 176 minutes to cover every 35 miles of the city. If the city is 70 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about a fog bank rolling into a city. It takes 176 minutes to cover every 35 miles of the city. The city is 70 miles wide from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge. We need to find out how long it will take for the fog bank to cover the whole city.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the problem. So, the fog covers 35 miles in 176 minutes. The city is 70 miles wide. So, the question is, how does the coverage rate work? Is the fog spreading uniformly across the city? Or is it moving along the length of the city, covering 35 miles while moving 70 miles? Hmm, maybe I need to visualize this.\n\nLet me think. If the city is 70 miles from one edge to the other, and the fog takes 176 minutes to cover 35 miles, maybe the fog is moving along the length of the city, covering 35 miles while the city itself is 70 miles wide. Wait, that might not make sense. Alternatively, maybe the fog is moving across the city, covering 35 miles in 176 minutes. But the city is 70 miles across. So, perhaps the fog is moving along the length of the city, covering 35 miles in 176 minutes. But the city is 70 miles long. So, if the fog is moving along the length, then to cover the entire city, which is 70 miles, it would take longer. But how much longer?\n\nWait, maybe the problem is similar to a moving walkway. If the fog is covering 35 miles in 176 minutes, then perhaps the speed of the fog is 35 miles per 176 minutes. So, we can find the speed in miles per minute, then see how long it takes to cover 70 miles.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fog is moving across the city, but the city is moving? No, the city is fixed. The problem says the fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. So, the city is stationary, and the fog is moving towards the city. The fog covers 35 miles in 176 minutes. So, the distance covered by the fog is 35 miles in 176 minutes. The city is 70 miles wide. So, if the fog is moving towards the city, covering 35 miles in 176 minutes, then how long would it take to cover the entire 70-mile city?\n\nWait, but if the fog is moving towards the city, then the entire 70-mile city would be covered once the fog has moved 70 miles. But the fog is moving at a rate where it covers 35 miles in 176 minutes. So, the time to cover 70 miles would be (70/35) times 176 minutes. Because if 35 miles takes 176 minutes, then 70 miles would take twice as long. So, 176 * 2 = 352 minutes. Is that the answer?\n\nBut let me check again. Let's compute the speed. If 35 miles take 176 minutes, then the speed is 35/176 miles per minute. Then, the time to cover 70 miles would be 70 divided by (35/176). Let's compute that. 70 divided by (35/176) is 70 * (176/35). Simplify 70/35 to 2, so 2 * 176 = 352. So, 352 minutes. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the fog is moving along the length of the city, which is 70 miles. So, if it takes 176 minutes to cover 35 miles, then the speed is 35/176 mph or something. Then, the time to cover 70 miles would be 70 divided by (35/176). Which again is 352 minutes. So, same answer.\n\nBut let me make sure there's no trick here. The problem says \"cover every 35 miles of the city.\" So, maybe the fog is covering 35 miles in 176 minutes, but the city is 70 miles in length. So, if the fog is moving along the length, then to cover the entire city, which is 70 miles, it would take (70/35)*176 minutes. Which is 2*176=352. So, that's the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the fog is covering 35 miles while the city is moving? But the problem doesn't mention the city moving. It just says the fog covers 35 miles in 176 minutes. The city is 70 miles across. So, I think the answer is 352 minutes.\n\nWait, but let me check units. 35 miles in 176 minutes. So, speed is 35/176 miles per minute. Then, time to cover 70 miles is 70 / (35/176) = 70 * (176/35) = 2 * 176 = 352. Yes, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that the fog is moving across the city, which is 70 miles wide. So, if the fog is moving at a rate such that it covers 35 miles in 176 minutes, then the time to cover 70 miles would be (70/35)*176 = 352 minutes. So, same answer.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 352 minutes.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe fog bank will take \\boxed{352} minutes to cover the whole city.\n</think>\n\nThe problem states that a fog bank takes 176 minutes to cover 35 miles of a city. The city is 70 miles wide. To determine how long it will take for the fog bank to cover the entire city, we need to calculate the time required to cover 70 miles given the rate of 35 miles per 176 minutes.\n\nFirst, we find the time per mile:\n\\[\n\\text{Time per mile} = \\frac{176 \\text{ minutes}}{35 \\text{ miles}} = \\frac{176}{35} \\text{ minutes per mile}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the total time required to cover 70 miles:\n\\[\n\\text{Total time} = 70 \\text{ miles} \\times \\frac{176}{35} \\text{ minutes per mile} = 70 \\times \\frac{176}{35}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the calculation:\n\\[\n70 \\div 35 = 2 \\quad \\text{so,} \\quad 2 \\times 176 = 352\n\\]\n\nThus, the fog bank will take \\boxed{352} minutes to cover the whole city.", "model_answer": 352.0, "ground_truth": 352.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 52, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 63 minutes to cover every 13 miles of the city. If the city is 39 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 63 minutes to cover every 13 miles of the city. If the city is 39 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about a fog bank rolling into a city. It takes 63 minutes to cover every 13 miles of the city. The city is 39 miles wide from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge. We need to find out how long it will take for the fog bank to cover the whole city.\n\nHmm, first, I need to visualize this. Imagine a city that's 39 miles long from the sea to the inland part. The fog is rolling in from the ocean, and it takes 63 minutes to cover each 13-mile stretch. So, the question is, how does the fog spread over the entire city, and how long does that take?\n\nLet me break it down. If the fog covers 13 miles in 63 minutes, then maybe the rate is 13 miles per 63 minutes. But wait, the city is 39 miles long. So, how many segments of 13 miles are there in 39 miles? Let me calculate that. 39 divided by 13 is 3. So, there are three 13-mile segments in the city. That means the fog needs to cover three times 13 miles, which is 39 miles total.\n\nBut if each 13-mile segment takes 63 minutes, then each segment is 63 minutes. So, if there are three segments, the total time would be 63 multiplied by 3. Let me check that. 63 times 3 is 189 minutes. So, is the answer 189 minutes?\n\nWait, but maybe I need to consider the rate of the fog's spread. Let me think again. If the fog covers 13 miles in 63 minutes, then the speed is 13 miles per 63 minutes. So, speed equals distance over time. So, speed = 13/63 miles per minute. Then, to find the total time for 39 miles, we can use time = distance / speed. So, time = 39 / (13/63). Let me compute that.\n\nDividing by a fraction is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal. So, 39 times 63 divided by 13. Let's compute 39 divided by 13 first. 13 times 3 is 39, so 39 divided by 13 is 3. Then, 3 times 63 is 189. So, that's the same answer as before, 189 minutes. So, that seems consistent.\n\nBut let me make sure there's no trick here. The problem says the fog bank is rolling from the ocean to cover the city. So, maybe the entire city is moving? Wait, no. The city is fixed. The fog is moving towards the city. So, the distance the fog needs to cover is 39 miles, and it's moving at a rate of 13 miles every 63 minutes. So, the time per mile would be 63 minutes divided by 13 miles. Then, for 39 miles, it's 39 times (63/13). Let me compute that.\n\n63 divided by 13 is approximately 4.846 minutes per mile. Then, 39 times 4.846 is approximately 39 * 4.846. Let me compute that. 40 * 4.846 is 193.84, subtract 1 * 4.846, which is 193.84 - 4.846 = 188.994, which is roughly 189 minutes. So, that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is about the entire city being covered, so the front of the fog needs to travel the entire 39 miles. If the fog moves at 13 miles per 63 minutes, then the speed is 13/63 miles per minute. The time to cover 39 miles would be 39 divided by (13/63) = 39 * (63/13) = 3 * 63 = 189 minutes. Yep, same answer.\n\nWait, but let me think again. If the fog is moving from the ocean to cover the city, is there a possibility that the entire city is moving? But the problem states that the city is 39 miles across from oceanfront to opposite inland edge. So, the city is stationary. The fog is moving towards the city. So, the distance the fog has to cover is 39 miles, and it's moving at a rate of 13 miles every 63 minutes. Therefore, the time is 39 divided by (13/63) = 189 minutes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is similar to a moving walkway. If a person takes 63 minutes to cover 13 miles, then the time per mile is 63/13 minutes per mile. So, for 39 miles, it's 39*(63/13) = (39/13)*63 = 3*63 = 189. So, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is about the entire city being covered, so the front of the fog has to travel the entire length of the city. If the fog is moving at 13 miles per 63 minutes, then the time to cover 39 miles is 39/(13/63) = 39*(63/13) = 189 minutes. So, that's consistent.\n\nI think that's solid. The answer is 189 minutes. Let me just check if there's another way to interpret the problem. For example, maybe the fog covers 13 miles in 63 minutes, but as it moves, the city is also moving? But the problem says the city is 39 miles across from oceanfront to opposite inland edge. That suggests the city is stationary. So, the fog has to cover a stationary 39-mile stretch. Therefore, the time is 39 divided by the rate of the fog. Since the rate is 13 miles every 63 minutes, the time is 39*(63/13) = 189.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is in different units? Wait, the city is 39 miles across. The fog covers 13 miles in 63 minutes. So, the time per mile is 63/13 minutes per mile. So, 39 miles would take 39*(63/13) minutes. 39 divided by 13 is 3, so 3*63 = 189. Yep.\n\nI think that's correct. All approaches lead to 189 minutes. So, the answer is 189 minutes.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe fog bank will cover the whole city in \\boxed{189} minutes.\n</think>\n\nThe problem involves determining how long it will take for a fog bank to cover a city that is 39 miles wide, given that it takes 63 minutes to cover 13 miles. \n\nFirst, we note that the city is divided into three segments of 13 miles each. Each segment takes 63 minutes to cover. Therefore, the total time to cover the entire city is calculated by multiplying the number of segments by the time per segment:\n\n\\[\n3 \\text{ segments} \\times 63 \\text{ minutes per segment} = 189 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the formula for time, which is distance divided by speed. The speed of the fog is 13 miles per 63 minutes, or \\(\\frac{13}{63}\\) miles per minute. The total distance is 39 miles, so the time required is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Time} = \\frac{39 \\text{ miles}}{\\frac{13}{63} \\text{ miles per minute}} = 39 \\times \\frac{63}{13} = 3 \\times 63 = 189 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nThus, the fog bank will cover the whole city in \\(\\boxed{189}\\) minutes.", "model_answer": 189.0, "ground_truth": 189.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 53, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 270 minutes to cover every 22 miles of the city. If the city is 66 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 270 minutes to cover every 22 miles of the city. If the city is 66 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about a fog bank rolling into a city. It takes 270 minutes to cover every 22 miles of the city. The city is 66 miles wide from the oceanfront to the opposite edge. We need to find out how long it will take for the fog to cover the whole city. Hmm, let me break this down.\n\nFirst, the fog covers 22 miles in 270 minutes. So, maybe I need to find the rate at which the fog is moving. If it's moving along the length of the city, which is 66 miles, then the time it takes to cover the entire city would depend on how fast the fog is moving across the width.\n\nWait, but the problem says it takes 270 minutes to cover every 22 miles. So, does that mean that the entire 22 miles is covered in 270 minutes? Or is it that each 22-mile segment takes 270 minutes? Hmm, the wording is a bit unclear. Let me read it again.\n\n\"A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 270 minutes to cover every 22 miles of the city. If the city is 66 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?\"\n\nSo, \"cover every 22 miles of the city\" in 270 minutes. So, the entire 22 miles is covered in 270 minutes. So, the rate is 22 miles per 270 minutes. Then, the city is 66 miles wide, so how long would it take to cover 66 miles at the same rate?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 22 miles is the length that's covered in 270 minutes, but the city is 66 miles in width. So, if the fog is moving along the width of the city, which is 66 miles, then the time to cover the entire city would be (66 miles / 22 miles per 270 minutes) * 270 minutes. Wait, that might make sense.\n\nLet me think. If the fog takes 270 minutes to cover 22 miles, then the speed is 22 miles per 270 minutes. So, speed = distance / time = 22/270 miles per minute. Then, the time to cover 66 miles would be distance / speed = 66 / (22/270) = 66 * (270/22) = 3 * 270 = 810 minutes. So, 810 minutes. Let me check that again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 22 miles is the length that's covered in 270 minutes, but the city is 66 miles in width. So, if the fog is moving along the width, then the time to cover the entire city would be (66 / 22) * 270 minutes. Because for each 22 miles, it takes 270 minutes, so 66 miles would be 3 times that distance, so 3 * 270 = 810. Yeah, that seems to make sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 22 miles is the length of the fog bank as it moves, but the city is 66 miles in width. So, the time to cover the city would be (66 / 22) * 270 minutes. Either way, same result. So, 810 minutes. Let me verify.\n\nIf the fog covers 22 miles in 270 minutes, then in one minute, it covers 22/270 miles. To cover 66 miles, the time required would be 66 / (22/270) = 66 * (270/22) = (66/22)*270 = 3*270 = 810. Yep, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, think of it as how many 22-mile segments are in 66 miles. 66 divided by 22 is 3. So, three times the time it takes to cover 22 miles. 270 minutes times 3 is 810. That seems right.\n\nSo, the answer should be 810 minutes. Let me just make sure there's no trick here. The problem says \"cover every 22 miles of the city.\" So, maybe the entire city is 66 miles, and the fog is covering 22 miles at a time. So, if it's moving across the city, which is 66 miles wide, then the time to cover the entire city would be (66 / 22) * 270 minutes. Which is 3 * 270 = 810. Yeah, that seems to check out.\n\nI don't see any other way to interpret this. The key is that the rate is 22 miles per 270 minutes, so per mile, it's 22/270 miles per minute. Then, for 66 miles, multiply by 66. Alternatively, since 66 is three times 22, the time is three times 270. Either way, 810 minutes. So, I think that's the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe fog bank will cover the whole city in \\boxed{810} minutes.\n</think>\n\nThe problem states that a fog bank takes 270 minutes to cover every 22 miles of a city. The city is 66 miles wide. To find the time it takes for the fog to cover the entire city, we start by determining the rate at which the fog moves. \n\nThe fog covers 22 miles in 270 minutes, so the rate is:\n\\[\n\\text{Rate} = \\frac{22 \\text{ miles}}{270 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{22}{270} \\text{ miles per minute}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the time required to cover 66 miles at this rate. The time \\( t \\) is calculated by:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Rate}} = \\frac{66 \\text{ miles}}{\\frac{22}{270} \\text{ miles per minute}} = 66 \\times \\frac{270}{22} = 3 \\times 270 = 810 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nThus, the fog bank will cover the whole city in \\boxed{810} minutes.", "model_answer": 810.0, "ground_truth": 810.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 54, "question": "A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 12 minutes to cover every 4 miles of the city. If the city is 76 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A fog bank rolls in from the ocean to cover a city. It takes 12 minutes to cover every 4 miles of the city. If the city is 76 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite inland edge, how many minutes will it take for the fog bank to cover the whole city?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about a fog bank rolling into a city. It takes 12 minutes to cover every 4 miles of the city. The city is 76 miles across from the oceanfront to the opposite edge. We need to find out how many minutes it will take for the fog bank to cover the whole city.\n\nHmm, first, I need to understand the relationship between the distance the fog covers and the time it takes. The fog covers 4 miles in 12 minutes. So, maybe I can find out the rate at which the fog is moving. Let's break it down.\n\nIf 4 miles take 12 minutes, then the speed (distance per time) is 4 miles per 12 minutes. To find the speed in miles per minute, that would be 4 divided by 12, which is 1/3 miles per minute. Wait, 4 divided by 12 is 0.333... miles per minute. So, approximately 0.333 miles every minute.\n\nBut maybe it's better to think in terms of how much distance the fog covers per minute. So, 4 miles in 12 minutes means that each minute, the fog covers 4/12 miles, which simplifies to 1/3 mile per minute. Yeah, that's correct.\n\nNow, the city is 76 miles wide from the oceanfront to the opposite edge. So, the total distance the fog needs to cover is 76 miles. If the fog is moving at 1/3 mile per minute, then the time required would be total distance divided by speed. So, time = 76 / (1/3). Dividing by a fraction is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal, so 76 * 3 = 228 minutes. So, 228 minutes?\n\nWait, let me check that again. If the fog moves at 1/3 mile per minute, then in one minute it covers 1/3 of a mile. So, how many minutes to cover 76 miles? That would be 76 divided by (1/3), which is 76 * 3 = 228. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nBut let me make sure I didn't make a mistake in the initial calculation. The problem says it takes 12 minutes to cover every 4 miles. So, 4 miles in 12 minutes. So, per minute, it's 4/12 = 1/3 mile. So, that's correct. Then total time is 76 miles divided by 1/3 mile per minute, which is 76 * 3 = 228 minutes. So, 228 minutes is the answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think about how many 4-mile segments there are in 76 miles. 76 divided by 4 is 19. So, 19 segments of 4 miles each. Each segment takes 12 minutes. So, total time is 19 * 12 minutes. Let's compute that. 19 * 12. 20*12 is 240, minus 1*12 is 12, so 240 -12 = 228. Same answer. So, that's a good check.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 228 minutes.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe fog bank will cover the whole city in \\boxed{228} minutes.\n</think>\n\nThe problem involves determining how long it will take for a fog bank to cover a city that is 76 miles wide, given that it takes 12 minutes to cover 4 miles. \n\nFirst, we calculate the speed at which the fog is moving. Since the fog covers 4 miles in 12 minutes, its speed is:\n\\[\n\\frac{4 \\text{ miles}}{12 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ mile per minute}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the time required for the fog to cover 76 miles. Using the formula \\( \\text{time} = \\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\):\n\\[\n\\text{time} = \\frac{76 \\text{ miles}}{\\frac{1}{3} \\text{ mile per minute}} = 76 \\times 3 = 228 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this by considering the number of 4-mile segments in 76 miles. There are \\( \\frac{76}{4} = 19 \\) segments. Each segment takes 12 minutes, so the total time is:\n\\[\n19 \\times 12 = 228 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nThus, the fog bank will cover the whole city in \\boxed{228} minutes.", "model_answer": 228.0, "ground_truth": 228.0, "is_correct": true}
