 would be 60 divided by 15. That's 4. So, 4 segments of 15 miles each. If each segment takes 377 minutes, then total time would be 4 times 377 minutes. Let me calculate that. 4 times 300 is 1200, 4 times 77 is 308, so 1200 + 308 = 1508 minutes. So, 1508 minutes total.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I'm interpreting the problem correctly. The problem says, \"It takes 377 minutes to cover every 15 miles of the city.\" So, does \"cover every 15 miles\" mean that the entire 15 miles is covered in 377 minutes? That is, the rate is 15 miles per 377 minutes? Or is it that the entire city is covered in 377 minutes, and the 15 miles is part of that?\n\nWait, the problem says, \"It takes 377 minutes to cover every 15 miles of the city.\" So, perhaps the entire 15 miles of the city is covered in 377 minutes. So, the rate is 15 miles per 377 minutes. Then, the entire 60-mile city would take (60 miles) divided by (15 miles per 377 minutes) times 377 minutes. Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 377 minutes is the time to cover 15 miles of the city. So, the time per mile would be 377 minutes divided by 15 miles. Then, for 60 miles, it would be (377/15) minutes per mile times 60 miles. Let's compute that.\n\nFirst interpretation: 15 miles take 377 minutes. So, 60 miles would take (60/15) * 377 = 4 * 377 = 1508 minutes.\n\nSecond interpretation: 377 minutes per 15 miles. So, time per mile is 377/15 minutes per mile. Then, 60 miles would be 60 * (377/15) = 4 * 377 = 1508 minutes.\n\nEither way, the answer is 1508 minutes. So, that seems consistent.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The problem says, \"It takes 377 minutes to cover every 15 miles of the city.\" So, if you have 15 miles of the city, it takes 377 minutes. So, the entire 15 miles is covered in 377 minutes. Therefore, the rate is 15 miles per 377 minutes. So, the time to cover 60 miles would be (60 / 15) * 377 minutes. Which is 4 * 377 = 1508 minutes. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, if the 377 minutes is the time to cover 15 miles of the city, then the time per mile is 377/15 minutes per mile. Then, for 60 miles, it's 60*(377/15) = (60/15)*377 = 4*377 = 1508 minutes. Same result.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1508 minutes.\n\nBut let me just make sure there isn't a trick here. For example, maybe the fog is moving in a certain direction, and the time starts when the front of the fog reaches the oceanfront, and then it takes 377 minutes to cover 15 miles. But the city is 60 miles across, so maybe the time is calculated differently.\n\nWait, but the problem says, \"cover every 15 miles of the city.\" So, perhaps the entire 15 miles is covered in 377 minutes. So, regardless of the direction, the time to cover 15 miles is 377 minutes. Then, the entire 60-mile city would take 4 times that, which is 1508 minutes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the 377 minutes is the time to cover 15 miles from the oceanfront. So, the front of the fog takes 377 minutes to move 15 miles inland. Then, the entire 60-mile city would be covered in (60/15) * 377 minutes, which is again 4*377=1508 minutes.\n\nSo, either way, the answer is 1508 minutes. Therefore, the answer is 1508 minutes.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe fog bank will cover the whole city in \\boxed{1508} minutes.\n</think>\n\nThe problem states that it takes 377 minutes for a fog bank to cover every 15 miles of a city. The city is 60 miles wide. To determine how many minutes it will take for the fog bank to cover the entire city, we need to consider the rate at which the fog spreads.\n\nFirst, we note that the city is 60 miles wide, and the fog covers 15 miles in 377 minutes. Therefore, the number of 15-mile segments in a 60-mile city is:\n\\[\n\\frac{60 \\text{ miles}}{15 \\text{ miles per segment}} = 4 \\text{ segments}\n\\]\n\nSince each 15-mile segment takes 377 minutes to cover, the total time to cover the entire city is:\n\\[\n4 \\text{ segments} \\times 377 \\text{ minutes per segment} = 1508 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nThus, the fog bank will cover the whole city in \\boxed{1508} minutes.", "model_answer": 1508.0, "ground_truth": 1508.0, "is_correct": true}
