{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 305, "question": "Deshawn has 65 square yards of orange field. There are 6 oranges per square yard. Deshawn can harvest his oranges every 6 months. How many oranges can DeShawn harvest within a year?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Deshawn has 65 square yards of orange field. There are 6 oranges per square yard. Deshawn can harvest his oranges every 6 months. How many oranges can DeShawn harvest within a year?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So, the problem is about Deshawn harvesting oranges from his 65 square yards of orange field. Each square yard has 6 oranges, and he can harvest every 6 months. We need to find out how many oranges he can harvest in a year. Hmm, let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's figure out the total number of oranges he has initially. If there are 65 square yards and each square yard has 6 oranges, then the total number of oranges should be 65 multiplied by 6. Let me calculate that. 65 times 6... 60 times 6 is 360, and 5 times 6 is 30, so adding those together gives 360 + 30 = 390. So, he starts with 390 oranges.\n\nNow, the next part is about the harvesting schedule. It says he can harvest every 6 months. A year has 12 months, so if he harvests every 6 months, how many times does he harvest? Let's divide 12 by 6. 12 divided by 6 is 2. So, he harvests oranges twice a year.\n\nBut wait, does that mean he harvests 6 oranges per square yard each time? Or is the 6 oranges per square yard per harvest? Let me check the problem again. It says, \"There are 6 oranges per square yard.\" So, that's per square yard per harvest. So, each time he harvests, he takes all the oranges from all the square yards. So, each harvest is 65 square yards times 6 oranges per square yard. Which we already calculated as 390 oranges. But since he can harvest every 6 months, he does that twice a year.\n\nSo, total oranges harvested would be 390 oranges per harvest times 2 harvests. 390 times 2 is 780. So, he can harvest 780 oranges in a year.\n\nWait, let me make sure I didn't miss anything. Let me go through it again. Total oranges initially: 65 * 6 = 390. Correct. Then, since he can harvest every 6 months, and there are 12 months in a year, dividing 12 by 6 gives 2 harvest periods. Each harvest removes 390 oranges. So, 390 * 2 = 780. That seems straightforward.\n\nIs there any possibility that the oranges deplete after harvesting? The problem doesn't mention any drop-off or lifespan of the oranges. It just says he can harvest every 6 months. So, assuming he can keep harvesting the same number each time, even though in reality, oranges might not stay on the tree forever, but since the problem doesn't specify any reduction, we can take it as given that each square yard has 6 oranges at the start of each harvest period and can be harvested again after 6 months.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 780 oranges harvested in a year.\n\n**Final Answer**\nDeshawn can harvest a total of \\boxed{780} oranges within a year.\n</think>\n\nDeshawn has 65 square yards of orange field, with 6 oranges per square yard. To find the total number of oranges initially, we calculate:\n\n\\[\n65 \\text{ square yards} \\times 6 \\text{ oranges per square yard} = 390 \\text{ oranges}\n\\]\n\nSince Deshawn can harvest every 6 months and there are 12 months in a year, the number of harvest periods is:\n\n\\[\n12 \\text{ months} \\div 6 \\text{ months per harvest} = 2 \\text{ harvests}\n\\]\n\nEach harvest removes 390 oranges. Therefore, the total number of oranges harvested in a year is:\n\n\\[\n390 \\text{ oranges per harvest} \\times 2 \\text{ harvests} = 780 \\text{ oranges}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{780}\n\\]", "model_answer": 780.0, "ground_truth": 780.0, "is_correct": true}
