ets, each 8 kg: 15*8=120 kg.\n\n- 30 one 39-kg packs: Wait, \"thirty-one 39-kg packs\". So 30 +1 =31 packs. 31*39=1209 kg. Correct.\n\n- 17 sacks of hay, each 17 kg: 17*17=289 kg. Correct.\n\nTotal: 32+120+1209+289=1650 kg.\n\nDivide by truck capacity 110 kg per trip: 1650/110=15. So 15 trips.\n\nYes, that seems right. So the answer is 15.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe farmer needs \\boxed{15} trips to transport all the feed.\n</think>\n\nThe farmer needs to transport all the feed using a truck that can carry 110 kg per trip. Here are the key steps to determine the number of trips required:\n\n1. **Calculate the total weight of each type of feed:**\n   - **Apples:** \\(8 \\text{ boxes} \\times 4 \\text{ kg/box} = 32 \\text{ kg}\\)\n   - **Beets:** \\(15 \\text{ bags} \\times 8 \\text{ kg/bag} = 120 \\text{ kg}\\)\n   - **Corns:** \\(31 \\text{ packs} \\times 39 \\text{ kg/pack} = 1209 \\text{ kg}\\) (since \"thirty-one\" means 31)\n   - **Hay:** \\(17 \\text{ sacks} \\times 17 \\text{ kg/sack} = 289 \\text{ kg}\\)\n\n2. **Sum the total weights of all feeds:**\n   \\[\n   32 \\text{ kg} + 120 \\text{ kg} + 1209 \\text{ kg} + 289 \\text{ kg} = 1650 \\text{ kg}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Divide the total weight by the truck's capacity to find the number of trips:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1650 \\text{ kg}}{110 \\text{ kg/trip}} = 15 \\text{ trips}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the farmer needs \\(\\boxed{15}\\) trips to transport all the feed.", "model_answer": 15.0, "ground_truth": 15.0, "is_correct": true}
