. Okay, so small cubes use 3 grams.\n\nNow, let's sum up all the water used. Giant cubes: 120 grams. Medium cubes: 143 grams. Small cubes: 3 grams. Total water used is 120 + 143 + 3. Let me add those up. 120 + 143 is 263, and then 263 + 3 is 266 grams. So, he used 266 grams of water in total.\n\nBut wait, the original amount of water he had was 348 grams. So, the water left would be 348 - 266. Let me subtract those. 348 minus 200 is 148, minus 60 is 88, minus 6 is 82. So, 348 - 266 = 82 grams. Therefore, he has 82 grams of water left.\n\nLet me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. For the giant cubes: 10 * 12 = 120. Correct. Medium cubes: 13 * 11. Let's do 11*10=110, 11*3=33, so 110+33=143. Correct. Small cubes: 24*(1/8)=3. Correct. Total used: 120+143=263, 263+3=266. Total water left: 348-266=82. Yep, that seems right. I don't think I made any errors here. So the answer should be 82 grams.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe amount of water Tariq has left is \\boxed{82} grams.\n</think>\n\nTariq has 348 grams of water and wants to make ice cubes. The problem requires calculating the remaining water after making 10 giant cubes, 13 medium cubes, and 24 small cubes. \n\n1. **Giant cubes**: Each uses 12 grams.\n   \\[\n   10 \\times 12 = 120 \\text{ grams}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Medium cubes**: Each uses 11 grams.\n   \\[\n   13 \\times 11 = 143 \\text{ grams}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Small cubes**: Each uses \\( \\frac{1}{8} \\) of a gram.\n   \\[\n   24 \\times \\frac{1}{8} = 3 \\text{ grams}\n   \\]\n\n**Total water used**:\n\\[\n120 + 143 + 3 = 266 \\text{ grams}\n\\]\n\n**Water left**:\n\\[\n348 - 266 = 82 \\text{ grams}\n\\]\n\nThe amount of water Tariq has left is \\(\\boxed{82}\\) grams.", "model_answer": 82.0, "ground_truth": 82.0, "is_correct": true}
