er of baskets, subtract from total bananas. Which is exactly what we did. So, that's the standard approach for these kinds of problems. \n\nSo, the answer is 105. \n\nBut just to make sure, let's do the division again. 337 divided by 232. Let's see, 232 times 1 is 232. 337 minus 232 is 105. So, the remainder is 105. So, 105 bananas can't be placed. \n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use modular arithmetic. 337 mod 232. Which is the same as 337 - 232 = 105. So, 105. \n\nYes, that's correct. So, the answer is 105. \n\nI think that's solid. So, the number of bananas that won't fit is 105.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of bananas that won't fit is \\boxed{105}.\n</think>\n\nLuis places bananas on 8 baskets, each holding 29 bananas. To find out how many bananas he cannot place, we first calculate the total capacity of the baskets:\n\n\\[\n8 \\text{ baskets} \\times 29 \\text{ bananas per basket} = 232 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nNext, we determine how many bananas will not fit by subtracting the total capacity from the total number of bananas he has:\n\n\\[\n337 \\text{ bananas} - 232 \\text{ bananas} = 105 \\text{ bananas}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of bananas that won't be able to fit on the baskets is \\(\\boxed{105}\\).", "model_answer": 105.0, "ground_truth": 105.0, "is_correct": true}
