and the middle digit becomes 12. 12 - 3 = 9. Then the hundreds place: 2 - 2 = 0. So the result is 98. Yeah, that's correct. So 329 - 231 = 98. \n\nTherefore, Esteban won't be able to place 98 bananas on the baskets. \n\nWait, let me just verify once more. If he uses all 11 baskets, each with 21 bananas, that's 231. He has 329 bananas. So 329 - 231 = 98. Yep, that seems right. So the answer should be 98. \n\nI think that's it. The key steps were calculating total capacity and then finding the difference between the total bananas and that capacity. No complicated steps here, just multiplication and subtraction. Double-checking the arithmetic made sure there were no mistakes. So the final answer is 98.\n</think>\n\nEsteban can place a maximum of \\(21 \\times 11 = 231\\) bananas across the 11 baskets. With 329 bananas, the number that cannot fit is calculated by subtracting the total capacity from the total bananas:\n\n\\[\n329 - 231 = 98\n\\]\n\nThus, Esteban won't be able to place \\(\\boxed{98}\\) bananas on the baskets.", "model_answer": 98.0, "ground_truth": 98.0, "is_correct": true}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 642, "question": "James places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 22 olives. If he has 345 olives and 11 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. James places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 22 olives. If he has 345 olives and 11 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. James has 345 olives and 11 plates. Each plate can hold 22 olives. The question is asking how many olives he won't be able to place on the plates. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of olives he can put on the plates and then subtract that from the total number he has. That should give the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the plates. If each plate holds 22 olives and there are 11 plates, then the total capacity is 22 multiplied by 11. Let me do that multiplication. 22 times 10 is 220, and then 22 times 1 is 22, so adding those together gives 220 + 22 = 242. So the plates can hold a total of 242 olives. \n\nNow, James has 345 olives. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of olives he has. So that's 345 minus 242. Let me do that subtraction. 345 minus 200 is 145, then minus 42 more would be 145 - 42. Hmm, 145 minus 40 is 105, and then minus 2 is 103. So 345 - 242 = 103. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 22 times 11: 22 + 22 is 44, so 44 times 2 is 88. Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, 22 times 11. Let me do it another way. 11 times 20 is 220, and 11 times 2 is 22. So 220 + 22 is indeed 242. Okay, that part checks out. Then 345 minus 242. Let's subtract step by step. 345 - 200 is 145. Then subtract 40 more: 145 - 40 = 105. Then subtract 2: 105 - 2 = 103. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nSo the answer should be 103 olives that he won't