{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 643, "question": "Gabriel places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 29 olives. If he has 342 olives and 11 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Gabriel places olives on some plates. Each plate can hold 29 olives. If he has 342 olives and 11 plates, how many olives won't he be able to place on the plate?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Gabriel has 342 olives and 11 plates. Each plate can hold 29 olives. The question is asking how many olives he won't be able to place on the plates. Hmm, so I need to figure out the maximum number of olives he can put on the plates and then subtract that from the total number he has. That should give the number that can't fit. \n\nFirst, let me calculate the total capacity of the plates. If each plate holds 29 olives and there are 11 plates, then the total capacity is 29 multiplied by 11. Let me do that multiplication. 29 times 10 is 290, and then 29 more would be 319. So, 29 times 11 is 319. That means all the plates together can hold 319 olives.\n\nNow, Gabriel has 342 olives. To find out how many he can't place, I subtract the total capacity from the number of olives he has. So that's 342 minus 319. Let me do that subtraction. 342 minus 300 is 42, and then minus 19 more would be 23. Wait, 342 - 319. Let me check again. 319 + 23 = 342, so yes, the difference is 23. \n\nTherefore, Gabriel won't be able to place 23 olives on the plates. Let me just make sure I didn't make any mistakes here. The total capacity is 29*11=319. 342-319=23. That seems right. So the answer should be 23.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of olives Gabriel won't be able to place on the plates is \\boxed{23}.\n</think>\n\nGabriel has 342 olives and 11 plates, each holding 29 olives. To find out how many olives he cannot place on the plates, we first calculate the total capacity of the plates:\n\n\\[\n29 \\text{ olives/plate} \\times 11 \\text{ plates} = 319 \\text{ olives}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the total capacity from the number of olives Gabriel has:\n\n\\[\n342 \\text{ olives} - 319 \\text{ olives} = 23 \\text{ olives}\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of olives Gabriel won't be able to place on the plates is \\(\\boxed{23}\\).", "model_answer": 23.0, "ground_truth": 23.0, "is_correct": true}
