rst: 3 / 15. Then 3 divided by 15 is 0.2. 0.2 * 100 is 20%. Yep, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by scaling down. If there are 1500 puppies total and 300 spotted, then 1% would be 15, so 20% would be 300. That works. So 15 * 20 = 300. So that checks out. So the answer is 20%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe percentage of all the puppies that have spots is \\boxed{20\\%}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine the percentage of all the puppies that have spots, we first calculate the total number of puppies. Noah's dog has 1010 puppies and Isabella's dog has 490 puppies. Adding these together gives:\n\n\\[\n1010 + 490 = 1500\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the total number of spotted puppies. Noah's dog has 170 spotted puppies and Isabella's dog has 130 spotted puppies. Adding these together gives:\n\n\\[\n170 + 130 = 300\n\\]\n\nTo find the percentage of spotted puppies out of the total number of puppies, we use the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{300}{1500} \\right) \\times 100\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the fraction:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{300}{1500} = \\frac{1}{5} = 0.2\n\\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage:\n\n\\[\n0.2 \\times 100 = 20\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the percentage of all the puppies that have spots is \\boxed{20\\%}.", "model_answer": 20.0, "ground_truth": 20.0, "is_correct": true}
