.00. Then, add the bond paper. $180.00 + $23.00. Hmm, 180 + 23. 180 + 20 is 200, and then +3 is 203. So total is $203.00. \n\nWait, let me verify each calculation again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nRulers: 6 * $2.5. 2.5 * 6. 2*6=12, 0.5*6=3. 12+3=15. Correct. \n\nFolders: 10 * $16.5. 16.5*10=165. Correct. \n\nBond paper: $23.00. \n\nTotal: 15 + 165 = 180. Then 180 + 23 = 203. \n\nYes, that seems right. So Mateo spent a total of $203.00 on all his school supplies. \n\nI don't think there's any discount mentioned here, so we don't have to consider any sales or special offers. All the items are priced at their regular rates. So adding them up gives the total expenditure. \n\nJust to double-check, maybe I can add the amounts in a different order. For example, add the bond paper first. $23 + $165 = $188. Then add the rulers: $188 + $15 = $203. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, adding all three together: 15 + 165 is 180, plus 23 is 203. All methods lead to the same total. So I think that's solid. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be $203.00. \n\n**Final Answer**\nMateo spent a total of \\boxed{203} dollars on everything.\n</think>\n\nMateo bought 6 rulers, 10 folders, and a rim of bond paper. To find the total amount spent, we calculate each category separately and then sum them up.\n\n1. **Rulers**: \n   \\[\n   6 \\times 2.5 = 15 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Folders**: \n   \\[\n   10 \\times 16.5 = 165 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Bond paper**: \n   \\[\n   23 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\nAdding these amounts together:\n\\[\n15 + 165 + 23 = 203 \\text{ dollars}\n\\]\n\nThus, Mateo spent a total of \\boxed{203} dollars on everything.", "model_answer": 203.0, "ground_truth": 203.0, "is_correct": true}
