hich is 1 question. Similarly, for economics, which also has 8 questions, she gets 1/8 of 8, which is another 1 question. So, in total, from physics and economics, she gets 1 + 1 = 2 questions. \n\nTherefore, adding up the points from biology and the points from the other subjects, she gets 4 + 2 = 6 points. So, the answer should be 6. Let me just verify that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nTotal questions: 20. 20% biology: 0.2*20=4. Correct. The rest: 20-4=16. Divided equally between physics and economics: 16/2=8 each. So physics and economics each have 8 questions. Aisha gets 1/8 of each. 1/8 of 8 is 1. So 1 from physics and 1 from economics. Total from non-biology: 2. Biology: 4. Total: 6. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nWait, but let me check if \"an eighth of the physics and economics questions\" could be interpreted differently. Maybe it's an eighth of the total physics and economics questions combined? But the problem says \"physics and economics questions,\" so each. Since they are divided equally, each has 8. So 1/8 of each would be 1 each. If it were 1/8 of the total, that would be 1/8 of 16, which is 2. But the problem says \"of the physics and economics questions,\" which probably means each subject. So, 1 each. So, 2 total. \n\nTherefore, 4 + 2 = 6. So, Aisha is sure to get 6 points. That seems straightforward. I don't see any mistakes here. Let me just recap once more. Biology: 4 questions, all correct. Physics: 8 questions, 1 correct. Economics: 8 questions, 1 correct. Total correct: 4 + 1 + 1 = 6. Yep, that's correct. So the answer is 6.\n\n**Final Answer**\nAisha is sure to get \\boxed{6} points.\n</think>\n\nThe quiz has 20 questions. 20% of the questions are biology, which is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n0.2 \\times 20 = 4 \\text{ questions}\n\\]\nThe remaining questions are equally divided between physics and economics:\n\\[\n20 - 4 = 16 \\text{ questions}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{16}{2} = 8 \\text{ questions each for physics and economics}\n\\]\nAisha is sure to get all the biology questions correct (4 questions) and an eighth of the physics and economics questions correct. For each subject (physics and economics), she gets:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{8} \\times 8 = 1 \\text{ question}\n\\]\nAdding the points from all correct answers:\n\\[\n4 \\text{ (biology)} + 1 \\text{ (physics)} + 1 \\text{ (economics)} = 6 \\text{ points}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]", "model_answer": 6.0, "ground_truth": 6.0, "is_correct": true}
