ay. 369 + 200 = 569. Then 569 + 95. 569 + 90 = 659, then +5 = 664. So total is 664. Wait, so my first calculation was wrong. So 369 + 295 is 664. Then adding Tuesday's 738. So 664 + 738. Let's do that. 600 + 700 = 1300. 60 + 30 = 90. 4 + 8 = 12. So 1300 + 90 = 1390, plus 12 is 1402. Wait, that seems high. Let me check again.\n\nAlternatively, 664 + 700 = 1364. Then subtract the 12 he overadded? Wait, no. Wait, 738 is 700 + 30 + 8. So 664 + 700 = 1364. Then 1364 + 30 = 1394. Then 1394 + 8 = 1402. So total is 1402. Hmm, but let me verify once more. Maybe adding all three numbers at once.\n\n369 + 295 + 738. Let's group 369 + 738 first. 300 + 700 = 1000. 60 + 30 = 90. 9 + 8 = 17. So 1000 + 90 = 1090, +17 = 1107. Then 1107 + 295. 1107 + 200 = 1307. 1307 + 95 = 1402. Yeah, same result. So total pineapples picked is 1402.\n\nWait, but let me check if I interpreted the Tuesday part correctly. The problem says \"on Tuesday, he picks double the number of pineapples he did on Sunday.\" So Sunday was 369, so double is 738. That's correct. So the numbers are 369, 295, 738. Adding them up gives 1402. Hmm, seems right. Let me just confirm once more.\n\n369 + 295: 300 + 200 = 500, 60 + 90 = 150, 9 + 5 = 14. Wait, that's 500 + 150 = 650 + 14 = 664. Then 664 + 738. 600 + 700 = 1300, 60 + 30 = 90, 4 + 8 = 12. 1300 + 90 = 1390 + 12 = 1402. Yep, same answer. So I think that's correct. So the total number of pineapples Arash has is 1402.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of pineapples Arash has is \\boxed{1402}.\n</think>\n\nArash picks 369 pineapples on Sunday, 295 pineapples on Monday, and double the number he picked on Sunday (which is \\(369 \\times 2 = 738\\)) on Tuesday. To find the total number of pineapples picked, we add the amounts from each day:\n\n\\[\n369 + 295 + 738\n\\]\n\nFirst, add the pineapples picked on Sunday and Monday:\n\n\\[\n369 + 295 = 664\n\\]\n\nNext, add the result to the number of pineapples picked on Tuesday:\n\n\\[\n664 + 738 = 1402\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of pineapples Arash has is \\(\\boxed{1402}\\).", "model_answer": 1402.0, "ground_truth": 1402.0, "is_correct": true}
