ur episodes each.\n\n- On Saturday: watched 45-minute episodes.\n\n- On Sunday: watched 3-hour and 45-minute episodes.\n\n- On Monday: watched 2-hour episodes.\n\nSo, the days are Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, and Monday. So, five days. Therefore, Tuesday is not mentioned. So, the problem is missing Tuesday's TV time? But the total is 29 hours. So, how can we compute Saturday's episodes if we don't know Tuesday's?\n\nWait, maybe the problem is intended to have five days: Monday to Sunday, but the problem statement skips Tuesday. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal problem:\n\n\"Monday, he got to stay up late, so he watched five 2-hour episodes. If he watched 29 hours of TV in all, how many 45-minute episodes did he watch on Saturday?\"\n\nSo, the problem mentions Monday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday. So, five days. Therefore, Tuesday is not part of the problem. Therefore, the total hours would be the sum of Monday (10 hours), Thursday (5 hours), Friday (5 hours), Saturday (unknown), Sunday (3 + 45 minutes). Wait, but we need to convert everything to hours for consistency.\n\nWait, let's convert all durations to hours.\n\nFirst, Monday: 5 episodes * 2 hours = 10 hours.\n\nThursday and Friday: each 5-hour episode. So, 5 hours each. So, 5 + 5 = 10 hours.\n\nSaturday: let's denote the number of 45-minute episodes as x. Each episode is 0.75 hours (since 45 minutes is 0.75 hours). So, total hours on Saturday is 0.75x.\n\nSunday: 3-hour episode and 45-minute episode. So, 3 + 0.75 = 3.75 hours.\n\nSo, total hours:\n\nMonday: 10\n\nThursday: 5\n\nFriday: 5\n\nSaturday: 0.75x\n\nSunday: 3.75\n\nTotal: 10 + 5 + 5 + 0.75x + 3.75 = 29\n\nLet me compute the sum of known hours:\n\n10 + 5 + 5 = 20\n\n3.75 is added to that, so 20 + 3.75 = 23.75\n\nTherefore, total known hours are 23.75. So, the remaining hours to reach 29 are 29 - 23.75 = 5.25 hours.\n\nTherefore, 0.75x = 5.25\n\nSolving for x:\n\nx = 5.25 / 0.75\n\nCalculating that: 5.25 divided by 0.75. Let's see. 0.75 goe