{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 1169, "question": "Rania is knitting winter wear for her 4 grandchildren. They're quadruplets, so they're all the same size. She wants to make a sweater, cardigan, beanie, mittens, and booties for each of them. It takes 15 skeins of wool to make a sweater, 16 for a cardigan, 18 for a beanie, 13 for a pair of mittens, and 9 for a pair of booties. How many skeins of wool will she need to buy?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. Rania is knitting winter wear for her 4 grandchildren. They're quadruplets, so they're all the same size. She wants to make a sweater, cardigan, beanie, mittens, and booties for each of them. It takes 15 skeins of wool to make a sweater, 16 for a cardigan, 18 for a beanie, 13 for a pair of mittens, and 9 for a pair of booties. How many skeins of wool will she need to buy?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. Rania is making winter wear for her four grandchildren, and they're all the same size. So, each grandchild will need a sweater, a cardigan, a beanie, a pair of mittens, and a pair of booties. The question is asking how many skeins of wool she'll need in total. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out how many of each item she needs to make for four grandchildren. Let me list out the items again: sweater, cardigan, beanie, mittens, and booties. Each of these items is for one grandchild, right? So, for four grandchildren, she needs four of each item. Wait, but let me check the problem statement again. It says \"a sweater, cardigan, beanie, mittens, and booties for each of them.\" So yes, four of each. \n\nNow, the next step is to calculate the number of skeins required for each type of garment. The problem gives the number of skeins needed for each item. Let me list those numbers again:\n\n- Sweater: 15 skeins\n- Cardigan: 16 skeins\n- Beanie: 18 skeins\n- Mittens: 13 skeins\n- Booties: 9 skeins\n\nSince each grandchild needs one of each item, an