{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 1254, "question": "In Mrs. Garcia's class of 80 students, 30% of the class are swimmers. Out of the remaining class, 25% of the students are choir members or part of dancers. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away meet. How many students are leaving early?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mrs. Garcia's class of 80 students, 30% of the class are swimmers. Out of the remaining class, 25% of the students are choir members or part of dancers. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away meet. How many students are leaving early?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So, Mrs. Garcia's class has 80 students. 30% of them are swimmers. Then, out of the remaining students, 25% are either choir members or part of the dancers. Those three groups need to leave early to travel to an away meet. I need to find out how many students are leaving early.\n\nFirst, let me break this down step by step. The total number of students is 80. 30% are swimmers. So, I need to find 30% of 80 to figure out how many swimmers there are. Then, subtract that number from the total to find out how many students aren't swimmers. Then, of those remaining students, 25% are either choir members or dancers. So, I need to calculate 25% of the remaining students. That percentage would give me the number of students leaving early, right?\n\nLet me write that out in equations to make it clearer.\n\nTotal students: 80\n\nPercentage of swimmers: 30%\n\nSo, number of swimmers = 30% of 80.\n\nCalculating that: 0.30 * 80 = 24 students.\n\nSo, 24 students are swimmers. Therefore, the remaining students are 80 - 24 = 56 students.\n\nNow, of these 56 students, 25% are either choir members or dancers. So, number of students leaving early = 25% of 56.\n\nCalculating that: 0.25 * 56 = 14 students.\n\nWait, so 14 students are leaving early