{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 1256, "question": "In Mr. Smith's class of 80 students, 25% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 15% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mr. Smith's class of 80 students, 25% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 15% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So, the problem is about Mr. Smith's class of 80 students. First, 25% of them are basketball players. Then, out of the remaining students, 15% are either dancers or part of the debate team. These three groups need to leave early to go to a tournament. We need to find out how many students are leaving early.\n\nAlright, let's break this down step by step. First, the total number of students is 80. 25% are basketball players. To find out how many that is, I can calculate 25% of 80. Hmm, 25% is the same as a quarter, right? So a quarter of 80 is 80 divided by 4, which is 20. So, 20 students are basketball players. That means the remaining students are 80 minus 20. Let me check that: 80 - 20 = 60. So, 60 students are not basketball players.\n\nNow, the next part says 15% of the remaining students are dancers or debate team members. Wait, does that mean 15% are dancers and 15% are debate team members, or 15% combined? The wording says \"15% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members.\" Hmm, the way it's phrased, \"or\" might mean either one or the other. But in math problems, unless specified otherwise, \"or\" usually means the total percentage. So, 15% of the remaining 60 students are either dancers or debate team members. So, we need to calculate 15% of 60.\n\nLet me confirm. If it were 15% of each, they would probably say 15% are dancers and 15% are debate team members. But here, it's \"15% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members.\" So, that's 15% of the remaining 60 students. So, 15% of 60. Let me compute that. 15% is 0.15 in decimal. So, 0.15 multiplied by 60. Let's do 0.15 * 60. 0.1 * 60 is 6, and 0.05 * 60 is 3, so total is 6 + 3 = 9. So, 9 students. Therefore, 9 students are either dancers or part of the debate team.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"these 3 groups of students will need to leave early.\" Wait, hold on. The original problem says \"25% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 15% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today...\" Wait, so the three groups are the basketball players, dancers, and debate team members? But the basketball players were already 25%, and then the remaining 60% are split into dancers and debate team members, each being 15% of that remaining 60%. So, total groups are basketball players (25%), dancers (15%), and debate team members (15%). So, all three groups combined. So, the total number leaving is 25% + 15% + 15% of the original class? Wait, but no. Wait, the basketball players are 25% of the entire class. Then, the dancers and debate team members are 15% of the remaining 60% of the class. So, the total number leaving is 25% of 80 (basketball players) plus 15% of 60 (dancers or debate team). So, 20 + 9 = 29. So, 29 students in total are leaving early.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. Let me parse the problem again. \"As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mr. Smith's class of 80 students, 25% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 15% of the students are dancers or part of debate team members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?\"\n\nSo, the three groups are basketball players, dancers, and debate team members. The basketball players are 25% of the entire class. The dancers and debate team members are 15% of the remaining students after basketball players have been removed. So, first, remove 25% (20 students) from 80, leaving 60. Then, 15% of those 60 are dancers or debate team members. So, 15% of 60 is 9. So, total leaving is 20 + 9 = 29. Therefore, the answer is 29.\n\nBut let me check again. If 15% of the remaining students (which are 60) are dancers or debate team members, that's 9 students. So, total leaving are basketball players (20) plus dancers/debate (9), so 29. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is interpreted as 15% of the entire class are dancers or debate team members, but that would be 15% of 80, which is 12 students. But the problem says \"Out of the remaining class,\" so it's 15% of the remaining students, which are 60. So, 15% of 60 is 9. So, 20 + 9 = 29. So, 29 students in total. Therefore, the answer is 29.\n\nWait, but let me check once more. Let's do all the calculations step by step.\n\nTotal students: 80.\n\nBasketball players: 25% of 80 = 0.25 * 80 = 20.\n\nRemaining students: 80 - 20 = 60.\n\nOf these 60, 15% are dancers or debate team members.\n\n15% of 60 = 0.15 * 60 = 9.\n\nTotal leaving: 20 (basketball) + 9 (dancers/debate) = 29.\n\nYes, that seems correct. So, the answer is 29.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of students leaving early is \\boxed{29}.\n</think>\n\nIn Mr. Smith's class of 80 students, 25% are basketball players. To find the number of basketball players, we calculate 25% of 80:\n\n\\[\n0.25 \\times 80 = 20\n\\]\n\nThis leaves 60 students remaining. Out of these 60 students, 15% are either dancers or part of the debate team. To find this number, we calculate 15% of 60:\n\n\\[\n0.15 \\times 60 = 9\n\\]\n\nAdding the basketball players and the dancers/debate team members together gives the total number of students leaving early:\n\n\\[\n20 + 9 = 29\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of students leaving early is \\boxed{29}.", "model_answer": 29.0, "ground_truth": 29.0, "is_correct": true}
