nd robotics are 38, so total 68. So, that's the answer. But let me check again.\n\nWait, the problem says \"Out of the remaining class, 40% of the students are debate team members or part of robotics club members.\" So, remaining class is 125 - 24% of 125 = 95. Then, 40% of 95 is 38. So, debate and robotics are 38. Then, the total number leaving is soccer players (30) plus debate and robotics (38) = 68. So, 68 students. Therefore, the answer is 68.\n\nBut let me check once more. Maybe the problem is interpreted differently. If \"Out of the remaining class, 40% of the students are debate team members or part of robotics club members,\" does that mean that among the remaining students, 40% are in either debate or robotics? So, the total number in those two groups is 40% of the remaining 95, which is 38. Then, the students leaving are those 38. But the problem says \"these 3 groups of students will need to leave early.\" Wait, the three groups are soccer players, debate, and robotics. But according to the problem, 24% are soccer players, then 40% of the remaining are debate or robotics. So, the total number leaving is soccer players (24%) plus debate or robotics (40% of remaining). So, 24% + 40% of (125 - 24%). Let's compute that.\n\n24% of 125 is 30. Then, remaining is 95. 40% of 95 is 38. So, total leaving is 30 + 38 = 68. So, 68 students. Therefore, the answer is 68. So, the answer is 68. Therefore, the number of students leaving early is 68.\n\nBut let me check once again. Maybe the problem is saying that after removing soccer players, 40% of the remaining are in either debate or robotics. So, the students in debate or robotics are 40% of the remaining 95, which is 38. So, those 38 are leaving. But the problem says \"these 3 groups of students will need to leave early.\" Wait, the three groups are soccer players, debate, and robotics.", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 68.0, "is_correct": false}
