{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 1270, "question": "In Mr. Smith's class of 100 students, 15% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mr. Smith's class of 100 students, 15% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So, the problem is about Mr. Smith's class of 100 students. First, 15% of them are basketball players. Then, out of the remaining students, 20% are either dancers or part of the choir. These groups need to leave early to go to a tournament. We need to find out how many students are leaving early.\n\nAlright, let's break this down step by step. First, the total number of students is 100. 15% are basketball players. So, to find out how many that is, I need to calculate 15% of 100. Hmm, 15% is the same as 0.15 in decimal form. So, 0.15 multiplied by 100 equals 15. So, 15 students are basketball players. That means the remaining students are 100 minus 15. Let me check that: 100 - 15 = 85. So, 85 students are not basketball players.\n\nNow, the next part says that 20% of the remaining students are dancers or choir members. Wait, does that mean 20% are dancers and 20% are choir members, or 20% combined? The wording says \"20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members.\" Hmm, the way it's phrased, \"or\" might mean either one or the other, but since they are separate groups, maybe it's 20% each? Wait, but the problem says \"these 3 groups of students will need to leave early.\" Wait, hold on,