embers. But the basketball players were already 15%, and then dancers and choir members are 20% of the remaining 85. So, total students leaving early would be 15% (basketball) plus 20% of 85 (dancers or choir). Wait, but the problem says \"These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today.\" So, maybe all three groups (basketball, dancers, choir) are leaving? But the wording is a bit confusing.\n\nWait, let me read the problem again carefully:\n\n\"As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mr. Smith's class of 100 students, 15% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?\"\n\nWait, so the problem says that 15% are basketball players. Then, out of the remaining students (which is 85), 20% are dancers or choir members. Then, these 3 groups (basketball, dancers, choir) will leave. Wait, but the problem says \"Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members.\" So, that 20% is of the remaining class (85 students). So, 20% of 85 is 17 students. So, 17 students are either dancers or choir members. But then, the problem says \"These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today.\" So, the three groups would be basketball players (15), dancers (17), and choir members (17)? Wait, but 15 + 17 + 17 = 49. But the total class is 100. That doesn't add up. Wait, maybe I'm misunderstanding.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that \"Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members.\" So, that 20% is a percentage of the remaining class (85 students). So, 20% of 85 is 17. So, 17 students are in either dance or choir. But then, the problem says \"These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today.\" So, maybe the three groups are basketball players, dancers, and choir members. But if 15% are basketball players, and 20% of the remaining are dancers or choir, then total leaving early would be 15% + 20% of remaining. Wait, but 15% of 100 is 15, and 20% of 85 is 17, so total is 15 + 17 = 32. But the problem says \"these 3 groups,\" which might imply three separate groups. Wait, maybe the problem is that \"Out of the remaining class, 20% are dancers or part of choir members,\" meaning that among the remaining 85, 20% are dancers and 20% are choir members. So, 10% each? Because \"or\" might mean split between the two. Wait, but the problem says \"20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members.\" So, that 20% is the total for both categories. So, 20% of 85 is 17. So, 17 students. Then, the three groups would be basketball players (15), dancers (17), and choir members (17). But 15 + 17 + 17 = 49. That's not 100. So, that can't be. Therefore, my initial interpretation must be wrong.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that \"Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members,\" meaning that 20% are in either dance or choir, but not necessarily split between the two. So, 20% of 85 is 17. So, 17 students. Then, the three groups are basketball players (15), dancers (17), and choir members (0). But that doesn't make sense. Alternatively, maybe the problem is that \"Out of the remaining class, 20% are dancers or part of choir members,\" meaning that 20% are in dance, and 20% are in choir, but not overlapping. But 20% + 20% = 40%, but the problem says \"20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members.\" Hmm, this is confusing.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is translated incorrectly. Let me check again. The original problem says: \"Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are dancers or part of choir members.\" So, in the remaining class (which is 85 students), 20% are in either dance or choir. So, 20% of 85 is 17. So, 17 students. Then, the three groups are basketball players (15), dancers (17), and choir members (0). But that adds up to 15 + 17 + 0 = 32. But the total class is 100. So, that can't be.", "model_answer": 100.0, "ground_truth": 32.0, "is_correct": false}
{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 1271, "question": "In Dr. Lee's class of 125 students, 40% of the class are volleyball players. Out of the remaining class, 40% of the students are robotics club members or part of choir members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away performance. How many students are leaving early?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Dr. Lee's class of 125 students, 40% of the class are volleyball players. Out of the remaining class, 40% of the students are robotics club members or part of choir members. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away performance. How many students are leaving early?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So, Dr. Lee's class has 125 students. First, 40% of them are volleyball players. Hmm, I need to find out how many that is. To find a percentage of a number, I multiply the percentage as a decimal by the total number. So 40% is 0.4, right? So 0.4 times 125. Let me calculate that. 0.4 * 125. Well, 0.1 * 125 is 12.5, so 0.4 is four times that, which is 50. So 50 students are volleyball players. That means the remaining students are 125 - 50. Let me check: 125 - 50 is 75. So 75 students are not volleyball players.\n\nNow, the problem says that out of these remaining 75 students, 40% are either robotics club members or part of the choir. Wait, does that mean 40% are in one of those two groups, or 40% each? The wording says \"40% of the remaining class are robotics club members or part of choir members.\" Hmm, the way it's phrased could be interpreted as 40% are in robotics and 40% are in choir, but that might not make sense because they can't both be 40% of the same group. Alternatively, it might mean that 40% of the remaining students are in either robotics or choir, possibly overlapping. But the problem says \"or part of choir members,\" so maybe it's two separate groups. W