m says \"out of the remaining class,\" which is 72. So, if it's 25% of the remaining 72, that's 18. So, that would be 18 students. But if it's 25% of the original class, that would be 20 students. But the problem says \"out of the remaining class,\" so it's 25% of 72. So, 18. Therefore, 18 students. So, the answer is 18. But let me check once more.\n\nWait, the problem says \"the remaining class\" after the swimmers. So, first subtract the swimmers: 80 - 8 = 72. Then, of those 72, 25% are choir or robotics. So, 25% of 72 is 18. So, 18 students. Therefore, the answer is 18. That seems correct.\n\nBut let me verify with another approach. Let's suppose that 10% are swimmers: 8 students. So, 72 left. Then, 25% of 72 are in either choir or robotics. So, 25% is 18. So, 18 students. Therefore, 18. Alternatively, if the problem had said 25% are in choir and 25% in robotics, but that would require 20 each, which is 40 total. But 40 is 50% of 80, which is not the remaining. So, that can't be. Therefore, the correct answer is 18. So, 18 students are leaving early.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of students leaving early is \\boxed{18}.\n</think>\n\nMrs. Garcia's class has 80 students. 10% of the class are swimmers, which is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n80 \\times 0.10 = 8 \\text{ students}\n\\]\nThis leaves 72 students remaining. Out of these 72 students, 25% are either choir members or part of the robotics club. To find 25% of 72:\n\\[\n72 \\times 0.25 = 18\n\\]\nTherefore, the number of students leaving early is \\boxed{18}.", "model_answer": 18.0, "ground_truth": 26.0, "is_correct": false}
