{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 1289, "question": "In Mr. Smith's class of 75 students, 40% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are choir members or part of dancers. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mr. Smith's class of 75 students, 40% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are choir members or part of dancers. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So, the problem is about Mr. Smith's class of 75 students. First, 40% of them are basketball players. Then, out of the remaining students, 20% are either choir members or part of the dancers. These three groups need to leave early to go to a tournament. We need to find out how many students are leaving early.\n\nAlright, let's break this down step by step. First, the total number of students is 75. 40% are basketball players. So, I need to find 40% of 75 to figure out how many are basketball players. Then, subtract that number from the total to find out how many students are left. After that, 20% of those remaining are either choir members or dancers. So, I need to calculate 20% of the remaining students. That should give me the number of students leaving early.\n\nLet me write this out mathematically. \n\nTotal students: 75\n\nBasketball players: 40% of 75 = 0.40 * 75\n\nLet me compute that. 0.40 times 75. Hmm, 0.4 times 70 is 28, and 0.4 times 5 is 2, so 28 + 2 = 30. So, 30 students are basketball players.\n\nTherefore, remaining students = 75 - 30 = 45.\n\nOkay, so 45 students are left after the basketball players have left.\n\nNow, out of these 45, 20% are choir members or part of dancers. Wait, the problem says \"these 3 groups of students will need to leave early today...\" Hmm, hold on. Wait, the original problem says \"40% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are choir members or part of dancers.\" So, does that mean that 20% of the remaining students are either choir members or dancers? So, the 20% is combined? So, the number of students who are either choir members or dancers is 20% of the remaining 45. So, that would be 0.20 * 45. Let me check that.\n\nWait, the problem says \"Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are choir members or part of dancers.\" So, \"of the remaining class\"—so the remaining class is 45 students. So, 20% of 45 is the number of students who are either choir members or dancers. So, that would be 0.20 * 45. Let me compute that. 0.20 * 45 is 9. So, 9 students. Therefore, the number of students leaving early is 9.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. The problem says \"these 3 groups of students\"—but then the next sentence says \"will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament.\" Wait, maybe I misread. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal problem: \"As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. In Mr. Smith's class of 75 students, 40% of the class are basketball players. Out of the remaining class, 20% of the students are choir members or part of dancers. These 3 groups of students will need to leave early today to travel to an away tournament. How many students are leaving early?\"\n\nWait, so first, 40% are basketball players. Then, out of the remaining class (which is 60%, since 40% are gone), 20% of those remaining are choir members or part of dancers. So, the 20% is of the remaining 60%, which is 20% of 60%, which is 12% of the original class. But the problem says \"these 3 groups\"—but the original problem mentions basketball players, choir members, and dancers. Wait, maybe the wording is confusing. Let me parse it again