12. Yeah, that's the same result. So that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can calculate the total number of emails in a week first and then take 80% of that. Let's see. A week has 5 days, right? So 160 emails per day times 5 days is 800 emails total in a week. Then 80% of 800 is 0.8 * 800. 0.8 times 800. Well, 0.8 times 8 is 6.4, so 0.8 times 800 is 640. Wait, that's different. Wait, hold on. Wait, no. Wait, 80% of 800 is 640. But 80% of 160 per day is 128 per day, which over 4 days is 512. But 800 total per week, 80% is 640. Wait, that's conflicting. Wait, no, maybe I messed up.\n\nWait, no. Wait, 160 emails per day times 5 days is 800 emails. Then 80% of 800 is 640. But 80% of 160 per day is 128 per day. So 128 per day times 4 days is 512. But 80% of 800 is 640. So why the discrepancy?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 80% of 160 per day is 128 per day. So over 4 days, that's 128 * 4 = 512. But 80% of the total emails in a week (which is 800) is 640. So why is there a difference?\n\nAh, because the 80% is not of the daily total, but of the total emails. Wait, no. Wait, 20% of the total emails are not responded to. So if he gets 160 emails each day, 20% of 160 per day is irrelevant. So over a week, the total irrelevant emails would be 20% of 160 * 5 days. Which is 0.2 * 160 * 5. Let's compute that. 0.2 * 160 is 32, then 32 * 5 is 160. So total irrelevant emails in a week are 160. Therefore, total emails in a week are 800, so 800 - 160 = 640 emails that require a response. So that's 640. But according to the daily calculation, 128 per day times 4 days is 512. Wait, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me check again. If he gets 160 emails a day, 20% are irrelevant. So each day, he has 160 emails, 20% irrelevant, so 80% respond to. So 128 per day. Then over 4 days, 128 * 4 = 512. But according to the weekly total, 160 * 5 = 800. 20% of 800 is 160, so 800 - 160 = 640. So why the discrepancy?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 20% of the total emails in a week ar