a room. So, each day, 40 rooms are added to the occupied rooms. Therefore, after 21 days, total occupied rooms would be 450 + 40*21. Let's compute that. 40*21 is 840. 450 + 840 = 1290. Therefore, unoccupied rooms are 1800 - 1290 = 510. So, same result. Therefore, the answer is 510.\n\nBut let me make sure there isn't a different interpretation. For example, maybe the 40 patients admitted each day are spread out over the existing rooms. But since the problem says \"admitted into the clinic each day,\" which might mean that each day, 40 patients are placed into the clinic, which could be either new or existing. But the problem doesn't specify whether existing patients are moved or if they just take new patients. However, the problem is about unoccupied rooms, so regardless of whether existing patients are moved or not, the total number of rooms needed would be the initial occupied rooms plus the new patients. So, even if existing patients are not moved, the number of rooms required would just be the number of patients. So, if each patient takes a room, then 450 initial patients plus 840 new patients would require 1290 rooms. Therefore, unoccupied rooms would be 510. So, that seems to make sense.\n\nAlternatively, if each room can hold multiple patients, but the problem doesn't specify, so we have to assume that each patient takes one room. Therefore, the number of rooms needed is equal to the number of patients. Therefore, the answer is 510. So, I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of unoccupied rooms after 3 weeks is \\boxed{510}.\n</think>\n\nThe clinic has a capacity of 1800 rooms with 1/4 occupied initially. Calculating the number of occupied rooms:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\times 1800 = 450 \\text{ rooms}\n\\]\n\nThe number of unoccupied rooms initially is:\n\n\\[\n1800 - 450 = 1350 \\text{ rooms}\n\\]\n\nOver 3 weeks (21 days), 40 patients are admitted each day. Calculating the total number of patients admitted:\n\n\\[\n40 \\time