cause the total occupied beds to exceed 1800. But 30 patients per day for 28 days is 840. 200 + 840 = 1040. 1040 is less than 1800, so the hospital hasn't reached full capacity. Therefore, the unoccupied beds would be 1800 - 1040 = 760. So the answer is 760.\n\nBut let me verify again. Let's check the math. 1/9 of 1800 is 200. Correct. 30 patients per day for 28 days: 30*28. 30*28 is 840. 200 + 840 = 1040. 1800 - 1040 = 760. Yes, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting a different approach. Let me think. Suppose each day, 30 patients are admitted, but the hospital can only accommodate 30 new patients, but existing patients might be leaving. But the problem doesn't mention anything about patients leaving. It just says 30 are admitted each day. So unless there's a different interpretation, I think the first approach is correct.\n\nWait, another thought: maybe the 1/9 occupied is a one-time thing. Like, the hospital is 1/9 full, so 200 beds, and then each day, 30 are admitted, which would make it 230 beds occupied. But the problem says \"after 4 weeks,\" so maybe over the 4 weeks, the number of occupied beds increases by 30 each day. So total occupied would be 200 + 30*28 = 200 + 840 = 1040. Then unoccupied is 1800 - 1040 = 760. So yes, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is considering that each day, 30 beds are added, but the hospital's capacity is 1800. So each day, 30 are admitted, but the total beds occupied can't exceed 1800. So perhaps on the first day, 30 are admitted, total occupied 230. Then next day, another 30, total 260, and so on. But even over 28 days, 30*28 = 840. 200 + 840 = 1040. Still under 1800. So even if the hospital hasn't reached full capacity, the unoccupied beds would still be 1800 - 1040 = 760. So regardless of whether the hospital has reached full capacity or not, the unoccupied beds would be 760. Because even if it hadn't reached full capacity, the unoccupied beds would be total capacity minus occupied. So even if the hospital was not full, the unoccupied beds would be 1800 - (initial occupied + daily admissions). So in this case, 1800 - (200 + 840) = 760. So the answer is 760.\n\nTherefore, the total number of unoccupied beds after 4 weeks is 760.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of unoccupied beds after 4 weeks is \\boxed{760}.\n</think>\n\nThe hospital has a capacity of 1800 beds with 1/9 occupied initially. Calculating the number of occupied beds:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{9} \\times 1800 = 200 \\text{ beds}\n\\]\n\nDue to the flu season, 30 patients are admitted each day. Over 4 weeks (28 days), the number of patients admitted is:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 28 = 840 \\text{ patients}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of occupied beds after 4 weeks is the initial occupied beds plus the patients admitted:\n\n\\[\n200 + 840 = 1040 \\text{ beds}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of unoccupied beds is the total capacity minus the occupied beds:\n\n\\[\n1800 - 1040 = 760\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of unoccupied beds in the hospital after 4 weeks is \\boxed{760}.", "model_answer": 760.0, "ground_truth": 760.0, "is_correct": true}
