rooms, they can accommodate 1330, so the number of unoccupied rooms would be 1600 - 1330 = 270. So, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that when they admit patients, they have to use the existing rooms? But that doesn't make sense. If you have 1800 rooms, and 200 are occupied, then you have 1600 unoccupied. If you admit 95 patients each day, then each day you need 95 more rooms. So, over two weeks, 1330 rooms needed. But since you have 1600 unoccupied rooms, you can accommodate 1330, so 1600 - 1330 = 270 unoccupied. So, same result.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 270. Let me check once more.\n\nTotal capacity: 1800 rooms.\n\nInitial occupancy: 1/9 * 1800 = 200 rooms.\n\nNumber of patients admitted per day: 95.\n\nNumber of days: 2 weeks = 14 days.\n\nTotal patients admitted: 95 * 14 = 1330.\n\nTotal occupied rooms: initial 200 + admitted 1330 = 1530.\n\nTotal unoccupied rooms: 1800 - 1530 = 270.\n\nYes, that seems correct. So, the answer is 270.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe total number of unoccupied rooms after 2 weeks is \\boxed{270}.\n</think>\n\nThe medical center has a capacity of 1800 rooms with 1/9 occupied initially. Calculating the initial number of occupied rooms:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{9} \\times 1800 = 200 \\text{ rooms}\n\\]\n\nOver 2 weeks (14 days), 95 patients are admitted each day. Calculating the total number of patients admitted:\n\n\\[\n95 \\times 14 = 1330 \\text{ patients}\n\\]\n\nAdding the initial occupied rooms to the patients admitted:\n\n\\[\n200 + 1330 = 1530 \\text{ occupied rooms}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the occupied rooms from the total capacity to find the number of unoccupied rooms:\n\n\\[\n1800 - 1530 = 270 \\text{ unoccupied rooms}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of unoccupied rooms after 2 weeks is \\boxed{270}.", "model_answer": 270.0, "ground_truth": 270.0, "is_correct": true}
