ount of the house.\" So, does that mean that the total loan was $500,000, or that he paid $500,000 towards the loan? The wording is a bit ambiguous. If the total loan was $500,000, then that's a deduction. If he paid $500,000 towards a loan that was more than that, then the deduction would be the full $500,000. But since the problem doesn't specify the total loan, maybe it's safe to assume that the $500,000 is the total deduction. So, total deductions are 28,800 + 28,800 + 500,000 = 557,600. Then, net proceeds are 960,000 - 557,600 = 402,400. So, $402,400.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the $500,000 is the total amount he paid for the house, which includes the transfer fees, brokerage fee, and the loan. But that would mean the selling price is $960,000, and the total cost is $960,000, which would mean he didn't make any profit. But that contradicts the question asking for net proceeds. So, that can't be.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the transfer fees and brokerage fee are part of the costs, and the $500,000 is also part of the costs. So, total costs are 28,800 + 28,", "model_answer": 28.0, "ground_truth": 402400.0, "is_correct": false}
