 what he has is 295 minus 190. Let me compute that. 295 minus 190 is 105. So Oscar is missing ¥105. \n\nWait, let me verify. Total cost is 590. Youth club pays half, so 295. Oscar pays the other half, which is 295. But he only has 190. So 295 minus 190 is 105. Yeah, that seems right. So the answer should be ¥105. \n\nBut let me make sure I didn't misinterpret the question. The problem says the youth club has decided to cover half of the cost. So the total cost is 590. Half of that is 295, which the youth club is paying. Therefore, Oscar needs to cover the remaining 295. But he only has 190. So he's missing 295 - 190 = 105. That makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is asking how much more money Oscar needs, which would be 105. So the answer is 105 yen. Yeah, that seems correct. I don't think I made any mistakes here. The key was understanding that the youth club covers half the total cost, so Oscar's contribution is half, and the difference between what he needs and what he has is the missing amount. \n\nAnother way to think about it: total cost is 590. Youth club pays half, so 295. Oscar has 190. So he needs 295 - 190 = 105 more. Yep, that's consistent. So I think that's the answer.\n</think>\n\nOscar needs to cover half of the music festival's total cost. The festival costs ¥590, so half of that is calculated as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{590}{2} = 295\n\\]\n\nOscar currently has ¥190. The amount he is missing is the difference between what he needs and what he has:\n\n\\[\n295 - 190 = 105\n\\]\n\nThus, Oscar is missing \\boxed{105} yen.", "model_answer": 105.0, "ground_truth": 105.0, "is_correct": true}
