/day. So total days = 27 / 9 = 3. \n\nAnother way to think about it: How much does each contribute per day. Yara: 3, Thiago: 6. Together, 9. Total needed: 27. 27 divided by 9 is 3. \n\nI think that's straightforward. Maybe the problem is testing whether I can interpret \"two times as much\" correctly. Sometimes people might misread that as Yara eating twice as much, but the problem says \"two times as much as Yara,\" so that's 6. \n\nAlternatively, if it had said \"twice the amount Yara eats,\" that would also be 6. Either way, same result. So, no confusion there. \n\nSo, the answer is 3 days. Let me just check once more. If on day 1, they eat 9, day 2 another 9, day 3 another 9. 9 x 3 = 27. Perfect. So, the cake will last exactly 3 days. \n\nI don't see any other factors here. The problem doesn't mention any other people eating or any other factors like the cake getting smaller or them not eating. So, straightforward division. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is 3 days.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe cake will last for \\boxed{3} days.\n</think>\n\nThe cake has 27 servings. Yara can eat 3 servings per day, and Thiago can eat 6 servings per day (since he eats two times as much as Yara). Together, they eat \\(3 + 6 = 9\\) servings per day. \n\nTo find the number of days the cake will last, we divide the total number of servings by their combined daily consumption:\n\\[\n\\frac{27 \\text{ servings}}{9 \\text{ servings/day}} = 3 \\text{ days}\n\\]\n\nThus, the cake will last for \\boxed{3} days.", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 3.0, "is_correct": true}
