in number of portions per day, but the total portions are 24. So, over how many days will the cumulative portions eaten reach 24. So, if Theo eats 2 per day, and Sakura eats 10 per day, then each day they eat 12 portions. So, 24 / 12 = 2. So, 2 days. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is more complex, like considering that they eat the pie together each day, so each day they eat 12 portions, and the pie has 24. So, 24 divided by 12 is 2. So, 2 days. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting me to consider that each person eats a certain number of portions per day, but the total portions are 24. So, over how many days will the cumulative portions eaten reach 24. So, if Theo eats 2 per day, and Sakura eats 10 per day, then each day they eat 12 portions. So, 24 / 12 = 2. So, 2 days. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting me to model it as a rate problem. Let's see. The pie is being eaten by two people. Theo's rate is 2 portions per day, Sakura's rate is 10 portions per day. Total rate is 12 per day. Total quantity is 24. So, time is 24 / 12 = 2 days. So, 2 days. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting me to use a different approach, like considering the combined eating rate. So, combined rate is 2 + 10 = 12 portions per day. Total portions 24. So, 24 / 12 = 2. So, 2 days. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting me to use a formula. Let me recall. If you have two people eating at different rates, the time taken to eat a total quantity is total quantity divided by the sum of their individual rates. So, time = 24 / (2 + 10) = 24 / 12 = 2. So, 2 days. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting me to consider that each day, the pie is being eaten by both Theo and Sakura, so each day, 12 portions are eaten. So, 24 divided by 12 is 2. So, 2 days. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting me