he problem. \"Viktor can eat 3 pieces a day while Arash can eat thrice as much.\" So Arash eats 3 times Viktor's amount. Viktor is 3, so Arash is 9. Together, 12 per day. 36 divided by 12 is 3. Yeah, that seems straightforward. \n\nI don't see any other factors here. The pizza is a fixed amount, they eat together, so the total consumption per day is additive. So 3 days is the answer. \n\nJust to make sure, let's think of it another way. Suppose Viktor eats 3 pieces each day, so in 3 days, he eats 9 pieces. Arash eats 9 pieces each day, so in 3 days, he eats 27 pieces. Together, 9 + 27 = 36 pieces. Exactly the whole pizza. Perfect. \n\nTherefore, the pizza will last for 3 days. I think that's solid. No mistakes in the calculations. The answer is 3 days.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe pizza will last for \\boxed{3} days.\n</think>\n\nViktor can eat 3 pieces of pizza per day. Arash can eat three times as much as Viktor, which means Arash can eat \\(3 \\times 3 = 9\\) pieces per day. Together, their combined eating rate is \\(3 + 9 = 12\\) pieces per day. \n\nThe pizza has a total of 36 pieces. To find out how many days the pizza will last, we divide the total number of pieces by their combined eating rate:\n\\[\n\\frac{36}{12} = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the pizza will last for \\(\\boxed{3}\\) days.", "model_answer": 3.0, "ground_truth": 3.0, "is_correct": true}
