ours. Thejanitor has 75 hours total. So, the question is, out of these 75 hours, how much is spent cleaning, and then what percentage is that cleaning time relative to the total day (75 hours).\n\nWait, but the problem says \"on average, what percentage of their day, on average, is spent cleaning floors?\" So, maybe it's just the total cleaning time divided by total time available, multiplied by 100 to get the percentage. Let me check.\n\nTotal cleaning time is 45 hours. Total time available is 75 hours. So, 45 / 75 = 0.6. Multiply by 100 gives 60%. So, 60% of their day is spent cleaning floors. Is that right?\n\nWait, but let me verify again. Let me go through each step again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nFirst, total time available: 15 hours/day * 5 days = 75 hours. Correct.\n\nTotal time needed: 90 floors * 30 minutes per floor. 90*30=2700 minutes. Convert that to hours: 2700/60=45 hours. Correct.\n\nSo, thejanitor spends 45 hours cleaning and has 75 hours total. So, 45/75 = 0.6, which is 60%. So, 60% of their day is spent cleaning. That seems straightforward.\n\nBut let me think if there's another way to interpret the question. Maybe they want the percentage of the day spent cleaning each floor? But no, the question says \"what percentage of their day, on average, is spent cleaning floors?\" So, it's total cleaning time divided by total day time, converted to a percentage. So, 60% seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they want to know how much of the day is spent cleaning each floor, but that would be a different calculation. Let me see. If they work 15 hours each day, and they need to clean 45 hours total, then per day, how much time is spent cleaning? But the problem says \"on average, what percentage of their day is spent cleaning floors?\" So, the entire day is 15 hours, and the total time spent cleaning is 45 hours. So, 45 hours cleaning / 75 hours total day = 60%.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they want to know how much of the day is spent cleaning 