e are 90 floors, each taking 30 minutes. So, per hour, how much of the building is cleaned? But that's not what the question is asking. The question is asking for the percentage of their day spent cleaning floors, not how much of the floor they clean per hour.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they want to know how much of the day is allocated to cleaning, considering the floors. But the problem states that they work 15 hours each day, so the total day is 15 hours. The total time spent cleaning is 45 hours. So, 45 hours cleaning over 75 hours total day. So, 60%.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is in units. Let me check units again. Time is in hours, floors are just floors. The rate is 30 minutes per floor. So, total time is 15 hours/day * 5 days = 75 hours. Total time to clean is 90 floors * 30 minutes = 2700 minutes = 45 hours. So, 45 hours cleaning / 75 hours total day = 60%.\n\nYes, that seems correct. So, the answer is 60%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThejanitor spends \\boxed{60\\%} of their day on average cleaning floors.\n</think>\n\nThejanitor needs to clean 90 floors with 5 days allocated, working 15 hours each day. The total time available is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n15 \\, \\text{hours/day} \\times 5 \\, \\text{days} = 75 \\, \\text{hours}\n\\]\n\nEach floor takes 30 minutes to clean, so the total time required to clean all 90 floors is:\n\n\\[\n90 \\, \\text{floors} \\times 30 \\, \\text{minutes/floor} = 2700 \\, \\text{minutes}\n\\]\n\nConverting this to hours:\n\n\\[\n2700 \\, \\text{minutes} \\div 60 = 45 \\, \\text{hours}\n\\]\n\nThe percentage of the day spent cleaning floors is then calculated by dividing the total cleaning time by the total day time and multiplying by 100:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{45 \\, \\text{hours}}{75 \\, \\text{hours}} \\times 100 = 60\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, thejanitor spends \\boxed{60\\%} of their day on average cleaning floors.", "model_answer": 60.0, "ground_truth": 60.0, "is_correct": true}
