{"dataset": "ds_main", "item_id": 1980, "question": "A janitor has to clean a office building with 120 rooms. They have 10 days to get it done. It takes them 15 minutes per room. If they work 6 hours each day, what percentage of their day, on average, is spent cleaning rooms?", "prompt": "As an expert problem solver, solve step by step the following mathematical questions. A janitor has to clean a office building with 120 rooms. They have 10 days to get it done. It takes them 15 minutes per room. If they work 6 hours each day, what percentage of their day, on average, is spent cleaning rooms?", "model_response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The problem is about ajanitor who has to clean 120 rooms in a building. They have 10 days to get it done. Each room takes 15 minutes to clean. They work 6 hours each day. The question is asking what percentage of their day, on average, is spent cleaning rooms. Hmm, so I need to find out how much time they spend cleaning each day relative to their total daily working time, expressed as a percentage.\n\nFirst, let me break down the information given. There are 120 rooms, each taking 15 minutes. So, the total time required to clean all the rooms would be the number of rooms multiplied by the time per room. That would be 120 rooms * 15 minutes per room. Let me calculate that. 120 * 15 is 1800 minutes. So, total time needed is 1800 minutes.\n\nNow, they have 10 days to complete this. But the question isn't about how many hours they work each day, but rather how much of their day is spent cleaning. Wait, but the problem says they work 6 hours each day. So, each day, they have 6 hours allocated. So, the total time they have is 10 days * 6 hours per day. Let me check that. 10 * 6 is 60 hours total. So, they have 60 hours to clean 1800 minutes of work.\n\nBut wait, maybe I need to convert everything into the same units. Let me confirm. The total work is 1800 minutes. The total time they have is 60 hours. So, to find the percentage of their day spent cleaning, I need to see how much of the 60 hours is used for cleaning. So, if they work 6 hours a day, and the total time available is 60 hours, but the work required is 1800 minutes. Wait, but maybe the 6 hours per day is the time they work each day, so over 10 days, that's 60 hours total. So, the total time they have is 60 hours. The total work is 1800 minutes. So, the time spent cleaning is 1800 minutes. But we need to express this as a percentage of their total daily working time, which is 6 hours.\n\nWait, but 1800 minutes is the total time required. So, if they work 6 hours each day, how much of that 6 hours is spent cleaning? But the total time they have is 60 hours. So, perhaps the 6 hours per day is the time they work each day, so over 10 days, that's 60 hours. So, the total time available is 60 hours. The total work is 1800 minutes. So, the time spent cleaning is 1800 minutes. But we need to find what percentage of the 6 hours they spend cleaning. Wait, but 1800 minutes is the total time required. So, perhaps the 6 hours per day is the time they work each day, so the total time they have is 60 hours. So, the time spent cleaning is 1800 minutes. So, the percentage would be (1800 minutes / 60 hours) * 100%. But wait, minutes and hours need to be converted to the same unit.\n\nAlternatively, maybe convert 1800 minutes to hours. Since 60 minutes = 1 hour, 1800 minutes is 1800 / 60 = 30 hours. So, total work is 30 hours. Total time available is 60 hours. So, the percentage would be (30 / 60) * 100% = 50%. So, they spend 50% of their day cleaning. Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, the problem says \"what percentage of their day, on average, is spent cleaning rooms?\" So, their day is 6 hours. The total time they have is 10 days * 6 hours = 60 hours. The total work required is 120 rooms * 15 minutes = 1800 minutes. So, converting 1800 minutes to hours: 1800 / 60 = 30 hours. So, the total work is 30 hours. So, the time spent cleaning is 30 hours out of 60 hours. So, 30/60 = 0.5, which is 50%. Therefore, 50% of their day is spent cleaning rooms.\n\nBut let me verify again. Let's see:\n\nTotal rooms: 120\n\nTime per room: 15 minutes\n\nTotal time required: 120 * 15 = 1800 minutes\n\nConvert total time required to hours: 1800 / 60 = 30 hours\n\nTotal time available: 10 days * 6 hours/day = 60 hours\n\nTherefore, the percentage of their day spent cleaning is (30 / 60) * 100% = 50%\n\nYes, that seems correct. So, the answer is 50%.\n\nBut let me check if there's another way to approach this. Maybe calculate how much time they spend cleaning each day, then find the percentage.\n\nTotal time required per day: Since they have 10 days total, but the work is spread over 10 days. So, per day, they need to clean 120 rooms / 10 days = 12 rooms per day.\n\nEach room takes 15 minutes, so per day, they clean 12 rooms * 15 minutes = 180 minutes. Convert that to hours: 180 / 60 = 3 hours per day.\n\nTherefore, each day, they spend 3 hours cleaning, and they work 6 hours each day. So, the percentage is (3 / 6) * 100% = 50%. Same result.\n\nYes, that's another way to look at it. So, per day, they have to clean 12 rooms (since 120 rooms over 10 days). Each room is 15 minutes, so 12*15=180 minutes=3 hours. So, 3 hours cleaning out of 6 hours working. 50%.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 50%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThejanitor spends \\boxed{50\\%} of their day cleaning rooms on average.\n</think>\n\nThejanitor needs to clean 120 rooms, with each room taking 15 minutes. The total time required is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n120 \\text{ rooms} \\times 15 \\text{ minutes per room} = 1800 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nConverting this total time into hours:\n\n\\[\n1800 \\text{ minutes} \\div 60 \\text{ minutes per hour} = 30 \\text{ hours}\n\\]\n\nThejanitor has 10 days to complete this work, and they work 6 hours each day. The total time available over 10 days is:\n\n\\[\n10 \\text{ days} \\times 6 \\text{ hours per day} = 60 \\text{ hours}\n\\]\n\nTo find the percentage of their day spent cleaning, we divide the total cleaning time by the total day time and multiply by 100:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{30 \\text{ hours}}{60 \\text{ hours}} \\right) \\times 100\\% = 50\\%\n\\]\n\nAnother way to verify is by calculating daily cleaning time. Thejanitor cleans 12 rooms per day (120 rooms over 10 days):\n\n\\[\n12 \\text{ rooms/day} \\times 15 \\text{ minutes per room} = 180 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nConverting this to hours:\n\n\\[\n180 \\text{ minutes} \\div 60 \\text{ minutes per hour} = 3 \\text{ hours/day}\n\\]\n\nThe percentage of the day spent cleaning is:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{3 \\text{ hours}}{6 \\text{ hours}} \\right) \\times 100\\% = 50\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, thejanitor spends \\boxed{50\\%} of their day cleaning rooms on average.", "model_answer": 50.0, "ground_truth": 50.0, "is_correct": true}
